## Proposed repair plan
# Repair plan for /playwright/playwright:<startup>

## Diagnosis

`docs/architecture/architecture.yaml` declares one service, `frontend`, at
`services/frontend` (typescript, next.js + react). **That directory does not
exist — there is no `services/` tree in the repo at all.** The only writable
root is `services/**` (`.claude/settings.json` allows exactly
`Write/Edit(services/**)`).

The harness chain is:

1. `tests/playwright.config.ts` derives a per-worktree host port from
   `sha256(process.cwd())` and sets `use.baseURL =
   http://127.0.0.1:<port>`.
2. Its `webServer` block runs `docker compose up --build frontend` with
   `cwd: '..'` (repo root), `env: { FRONTEND_PORT: <port>,
   COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME: bdd-<digest> }`, and waits up to 300 s for
   `http://127.0.0.1:<port>` to answer.
3. `docker-compose.yaml` defines `frontend` with `build.context:
   ./services/frontend` and maps `"${FRONTEND_PORT:-3000}:3000"` — i.e. **the
   app must listen on port 3000 inside the container**.

Because `./services/frontend` is missing, `docker compose up --build` aborts
immediately, nothing ever binds the port, and Playwright fails during
`webServer` startup. A startup failure yields no per-test results, which is
why the failing set surfaces as the synthetic test id `<startup>` with an
empty failure group.

So this is not a bug to patch — the entire production service has to be
built. Nothing in `tests/`, `docker-compose.yaml` or `docs/` may be touched;
all work lands under `services/frontend/`.

## Contract the service must satisfy

- **Buildable compose context.** `services/frontend/` must contain a
  `Dockerfile` that `docker compose up --build frontend` can build with no
  extra build args, no host-provided env, and no files from outside that
  directory.
- **Listens on 3000, on all interfaces.** The compose port mapping publishes
  container `3000` to the host port Playwright polls. The process must bind
  `0.0.0.0:3000` (not `127.0.0.1` inside the container, or the published port
  answers with a connection reset and Playwright times out after 300 s).
- **`GET /` returns HTTP 200** so Playwright's URL readiness probe resolves,
  and the container must stay up for the duration of the test run (it is a
  foreground `docker compose up`, not detached).
- **`GET /` renders exactly one `<h1>` whose text is `Hello world`.**
  `tests/integration/frontend-home.spec.ts` does
  `page.goto('/')` then
  `expect(page.locator('h1')).toHaveText('Hello world')`. `toHaveText` with a
  string is an exact match on normalised text, and the locator runs in strict
  mode — a second `h1` anywhere in the document (including one injected by the
  root layout or a dev-mode overlay) is a strict-mode violation. One `h1`,
  text exactly `Hello world`, no extra nested markup inside it.
- **Next.js App Router, server components by default.** The architecture's
  `component-composition` pattern says server components by default and
  `'use client'` only when hooks or browser APIs are needed. The home page
  needs neither, so no `'use client'` anywhere in this change.
- **`services/frontend/tsconfig.json` must exist**, because the architecture
  declares it as the `typescript` code quality gate's config.

## Files to create

All paths relative to the repo root. Every one of these is new.

### 1. `services/frontend/package.json`
Private package for the service. Scripts must include `build` and a `start`
that binds the right host/port, e.g. `next start -H 0.0.0.0 -p 3000`
(`-H 0.0.0.0` explicitly, so a stray `HOSTNAME` env in the base image cannot
make Next bind the wrong interface). Dependencies: `next`, `react`,
`react-dom`. Dev dependencies: `typescript`, `@types/node`, `@types/react`,
`@types/react-dom`.

Pin **exact** versions (no `^`/`~`) for `next`, `react` and `react-dom` so the
image build is reproducible and a future major cannot silently break the
startup path. Pick a Next 15.x release with the matching React 19.x pair it is
tested against, and a Node base image satisfying Next's engine floor (Node 20
or 22 — do not use Node 18).

*Serves:* `<startup>` (without this, the Dockerfile has nothing to install or
run).

### 2. `services/frontend/Dockerfile`
Keep it single-stage and boring — every extra stage is another way for
`<startup>` to fail. Intended shape:

- `FROM node:22-alpine` (or the chosen LTS alpine tag), `WORKDIR /app`.
- Copy the manifest(s) first, install dependencies, then copy the rest of the
  service and run `npm run build`.
- `EXPOSE 3000`, `CMD` running the `start` script.

Dependency install: if you also commit a `package-lock.json` (preferred —
generate it by running `npm install` inside `services/frontend/` and keep the
resulting lockfile), copy it alongside `package.json` and use
`npm ci --no-audit --no-fund`. If no lockfile is committed, use
`npm install --no-audit --no-fund` — do **not** write a `npm ci` that will
abort because the lockfile is absent.

Do not add `output: 'standalone'` plumbing; it adds copy steps that are easy
to get wrong and buys nothing here.

*Serves:* `<startup>` — this is the file whose absence currently kills the
run.

### 3. `services/frontend/.dockerignore`
Exclude `node_modules`, `.next`, and any local build output. The build context
is `services/frontend` itself, so if you generate a lockfile by running
`npm install` on the host, the host `node_modules` would otherwise be copied
into the image and can shadow the container install (wrong platform binaries
for `next`/`sharp` → build or start failure).

*Serves:* `<startup>` (robustness of the image build).

### 4. `services/frontend/next.config.mjs`
Minimal config object exported as default. Use `.mjs` plain JavaScript rather
than `next.config.ts`, to avoid depending on the TS-config loader.

*Serves:* `<startup>`.

### 5. `services/frontend/tsconfig.json`
Standard Next.js App Router tsconfig: `strict: true`, `jsx: "preserve"`,
`moduleResolution: "bundler"`, `module: "esnext"`, `target: "ES2022"`,
`lib` including `dom`/`esnext`, `allowJs`, `skipLibCheck`, `esModuleInterop`,
`isolatedModules`, `incremental`, `noEmit: true`, the `next` plugin, the
`@/*` → `./*` path alias, `include` covering `next-env.d.ts`, `**/*.ts`,
`**/*.tsx` and `.next/types/**/*.ts`, `exclude: ["node_modules"]`.

Also commit a `services/frontend/next-env.d.ts` with the standard
`/// <reference types="next" />` and
`/// <reference types="next/image-types/global" />` lines, so `tsc` has the
JSX/Next ambient types even on a checkout where `next build` has not yet run.

*Serves:* the architecture's declared `typescript` quality gate; indirectly
`<startup>`, since `next build` type-checks the app and a missing or
inconsistent tsconfig fails the image build.

### 6. `services/frontend/app/layout.tsx`
Required root layout for the App Router — without it `next build` fails. A
server component (no `'use client'`) exporting a default
`RootLayout({ children })` that renders `<html lang="en"><body>{children}
</body></html>`, plus an optional `metadata` export. **Must not render any
`<h1>`** or other heading, or the spec's `h1` locator becomes ambiguous.

*Serves:* `<startup>` (build correctness) and
`home page greets with Hello world` (keeps the `h1` locator unique).

### 7. `services/frontend/app/page.tsx`
The home route. Default-exported server component returning exactly one
`<h1>Hello world</h1>` — the literal string, correct capitalisation, as the
sole text content of that element. Wrap it in at most a `<main>`; add no
second heading.

*Serves:* `home page greets with Hello world`, the spec that runs once
`<startup>` is fixed.

